# K03d: the destination of the atom spelling is NOT a first operand, and it is not a new declaration
# when the name is already bound -- `w` is an int here, so `w: merge Model` is an assignment of a merge
# value to an existing binding, which the current receiving-context machinery refuses.  The point of
# this row is that BOTH surface forms refuse the SAME way: the frame-spelling twin `w: merge: Model`
# gives the identical diagnostic at the identical site.  The message is the assignment path's current
# one, not a claim of the language.
Model: (int: value 1)
fn: m () int
    int: w 5
    w: merge Model
    return: w\value
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: m(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
